 "rulebook": "World Athletics Competition and Technical Rules 2024: TR 32.5-32.8 (the circle, 2.50 m for the discus), TR 32.12 (the 34.92 degree sector), TR 32.13, 32.14, 32.16, 32.17 and 32.20 (throwing events: the start, failures, the landing, leaving the circle, the measurement), TR 34 (the men's discus), TR 35 (the discus cage)",
 "deviations": [
  "TR 34 (the discus; the thrower's hand): the right hand is the H1-2's own Inspire five-finger hand (six drives: four fingers, the thumb's flexion and rotation; fingertip forces 20 N, the thumb 30 N, at 1.0 times these forces; one closure command for the whole hand). The discus lies flat on its palm, the fingertips curled over the rim, held by contact and friction only: nothing is welded or grippable, and the discus slips, turns in the hand or flies out when the throw pulls harder than the fingers hold. The release is the hand's last hold (the discus touching the hand's fingers and its thumb or palm) before it is clear of the robot.",
  "TR 34 (the discus' shape): an ellipsoid of the official diameter, thickness and mass (no separate metal rim: its moment of inertia is that of a uniform body; near the rim it is 13 to 19 mm thick, as the rule book's rim).",
  "TR 35 (the cage): there is no cage.",
  "TR 32.5-32.6: the ground outside the circle is level with the rim's top for 1 m and then falls to the field's level over 1 m (the circle's inside is the field's level, 0.02 m below the rim, instead of the field being level with the rim).",
  "TR 32.20: the mark is the point where the discus first touches the ground.",
  "TR 32.15 (interrupting a trial, laying the implement down): not modelled; the first release is the throw, and the discus may not be gripped again.",
  "TR 34 / TR 32 (the throw is the hand's alone): the rule book has no article on touching the discus after it has left the hand; here the right hand may still brush it during 0.2 s after the release, but those touches may change its velocity by at most 0.5 m/s in all (the sum of their contact impulses over its mass): a harder touch is a second propulsion of a released implement, and fails the throw.",
  "TR 32.14 names no fall: here a fall ends the trial as a failure, as in every task of this benchmark; the trial ends when the robot has left the circle correctly and stands.",
  "TR 25.17 (one minute for a trial): the whole trial, leaving the circle included, has 60 s.",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model: drag, lift and the pitching moment of an ellipsoid, coefficients of the order of the published wind-tunnel data), still air, no wind.",
  "The qualifying mark of 15.0 m is this benchmark's (a school standard for the 2 kg discus); the rule book only measures."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Throw the discus that lies on your right palm, your fingers curled over its rim, from inside the circle so that it lands inside the sector at least 15.0 m from the circle, then leave the circle from its rear half and stand.",
  "rules": "The men's discus (2.0 kg, 0.22 m across, 0.045 m thick at its centre) starts flat on the palm of your right hand, the fingertips curled over its rim (the row's right grip value is the hand's closure command, 0 open to 1 closed; nothing is welded or grippable: contact and friction alone hold it, and it slips, turns or flies out when the throw pulls harder than the fingers hold). The circle is 2.5 m across inside its rim; the ground outside is 0.02 m higher than its inside. The sector opens from the circle's centre, 34.92 degrees wide, symmetric about the direction you face at the start. Holding: the discus touches the right hand's fingers and its thumb or palm. Before the release nothing of you but your right hand (the fingers, the palm and the wrist) may touch it, and it may not stay on the hand without the hand's hold for more than 0.2 s. The release is the hand's last hold before the discus has touched nothing of you for 0.03 s; there is one release: after it the discus may not be held by the hand again, nothing of you but the right hand may touch it, and the right hand only during the first 0.2 s (brushing it as it leaves), if those touches change its velocity by at most 0.5 m/s in all (the sum of their contact impulses over its mass: the release must be clean). Its first touch of the ground must be inside the sector lines (on a line is out); the throw is measured from that point to the inside of the rim along the line through the circle's centre. You succeed when a valid throw measures at least 15.0 m and you have then left the circle correctly and stood for 1.0 s. Leaving correctly: after the discus has landed, your first touch of the rim's top or of the ground outside the circle is behind the line through the circle's centre across the throwing direction (the rear half). Standing: only your feet on the ground, the torso within about 20 degrees of upright, the pelvis slower than 0.35 m/s. You fail if any part of you touches the rim's top (its top inside edge included) or the ground outside the circle before the discus has landed, or in the front half before you have left correctly; if the discus is touched by anything but the right hand before the release, stays on the hand without the hand's hold, is held again or touched after the release (but for the right hand's brush); if it lands outside the sector or short of 15.0 m; if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the circle's floor, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground); or after 60 s. Where you touch the ground is judged on the points of contact; a contact within 0.003 m of the rim's top counts as on it.",
  "venue": "You stand in the rear half of the circle facing the sector (+x), your pelvis 0.55 m behind the circle's centre. Your right forearm is level and points forwards, rolled palm up; the discus lies flat on the palm, the closed hand's fingertips curled over its rim. There is no cage. Still air.",
  "difficulty": "15.0 m needs a release at about 12 m/s near 35 to 40 degrees with the discus flat to its flight and spinning, or the air turns it over. The H1-2's arm motors give 40 N m at the shoulder, so the speed has to come from the trunk's turn, inside a circle of 2.5 m that the robot may not leave until the discus is down. At that speed the discus pulls on the hand with some 300 N, held only by fingertips curled over its rim and the palm under it: the hand's path has to keep the discus pressed into the palm and the fingers until the release."
 },
